[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"295","stub":{"id":"295","section":"2) Jones Beach to Klamath River","altname":"Scott River Canyon","river":"Scott","difficulty":"IVtoV","states":["USA-CAL"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"209","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"Access is an informal access site available on river right a short distance upstream of Boulder Creek Rapid. It is located where the river comes up against the road at mile 15.2 with convenient lowbank access.","id":"25KP9uYdE6pvHUT0uQOhp","location":{"latitude":"41.634908","longitude":"-123.09409"},"name":"Boulder Creek Access","distance":"0","photoID":"krBJrSF4gihaohgcbdp3u","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"This rapid is on a slight left bend and features a sizable hole in the middle. It is the last rapid before Boulder Creek Falls. This is also the last place to exit the river in this vicinity where the road is close to river level without steep banks to climb up.","id":"9YliTrBYBFyXqV3iHx17x","location":{"latitude":"41.635142","longitude":"-123.096154"},"name":"Unnamed Rapid","distance":"0.09","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"Boulder Creek Falls can't be missed! It's the largest, most congested rapid on the river and it is highly visible from Scott River Road. Most boaters put in below here to avoid it, but there's a technical line through the drop for hearty kayakers and small craft boaters. Boating this drop also sets you up for a fun run down Canyon Creek Rapid just downstream.","id":"94weRpF2IxgnsQyNyaQ5A","location":{"latitude":"41.634296","longitude":"-123.099494"},"name":"Boulder Creek Falls","distance":"0.28","difficulty":"V","approximate":false,"photoID":"OKGFgts7VHHlzIZPzDCrP"},{"type":"rapid","description":"A long rapid that spans the reach of river just upstream and just downstream of Canyon Creek, a significant tributary that enters on river left. Buker Bridge spans the river just below the end of this hole-filled rapid.","id":"gipmNgfEGi8tLPdkDcO68","location":{"latitude":"41.632671","longitude":"-123.102353"},"name":"Canyon Creek Rapid","distance":"0.47","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"access","description":"Buker Bridge is a good access point that, when used as a put-in, allows boaters to avoid Boulder Creek Falls located upstream but gain some additional whitewater that they would miss when putting in downstream at Bridge Flat. Buker Bridge is on Canyon Creek Road, a dirt road off Scott River Road. Unfortunately, the bridge is just downstream of Canyon Creek Rapid, a long and fun rapid that's quite busy.","id":"4fvmJquVCKFYtCyNuyg2w","location":{"latitude":"41.634011","longitude":"-123.105728"},"name":"Buker Bridge","distance":"0.67","photoID":"FrDjxj17xpYwASqo48Uh2","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"First rapid downstream of Buker Bridge.","id":"aPg3yYBoF8H4Rd4ipc1By","location":{"latitude":"41.634767","longitude":"-123.106551"},"name":"Unnamed Rapid","distance":"0.74","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"cmuy0FdE5GigKx3yQNJgZ"},{"type":"rapid","description":"Easily visible from Scott River Road.","id":"eVD7A2SjQnE8X5IqLA3o9","location":{"latitude":"41.63812","longitude":"-123.108507"},"name":"Unnamed Rapid","distance":"1","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"The river splits around an island. Most of the flow and all of the fun whitewater are on the left side.","id":"wKNCjHTk1RqDZd42vE5qc","location":{"latitude":"41.640841","longitude":"-123.112164"},"name":"Island Rapid","distance":"1.29","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"A long rapid just downstream of Kelsey Creek, a significant tributary that drains the marble high country and enters on river left. Be sure to eddy out behind some of the magnificant marble boulders and crazy rocks that form this rapid.","id":"MYqEeLj30A7tXVE9knuqA","location":{"latitude":"41.645986","longitude":"-123.115669"},"name":"Kelsey Creek Rapid","distance":"1.76","difficulty":"IV-","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"put-in","description":"Located in the heart of the river canyon, Bridge Flat offers access to the river left side of the Scott River on the downstream side of the Scott River Road bridge. There is also a small campground that is open year-round and is a great place to stay for multiple days of boating on the Scott. Siskiyou Telephone has a free public phone at the river access parking area. Use it to get the latest flow reading by calling [Waterline](http://www.h2oline.com/default.aspx?pg=st&op=CA) at 1-800-452-1737 and entering 063492 for the Scott River near Fort Jones.","id":"sRnIPzd1jUgihJguwIrB7","location":{"latitude":"41.648476","longitude":"-123.113271"},"name":"Bridge Flat River Access & Campground","distance":"2","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"There are several large rocks in this general area of river, but one is bigger than the rest. Kayakers: be on the lookout for good splats in this zone.","id":"v7i5hjcl8K3mayUcjvkNZ","location":{"latitude":"41.670975","longitude":"-123.105208"},"name":"Big Rock","distance":"4.01","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"","id":"iHoyDsM2Qv99wKXU6eddJ","location":{"latitude":"41.671918","longitude":"-123.10336"},"name":"Unnamed Rapid","distance":"4.12","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"","id":"SfR9BhRVhQ6TD56Hiq3q6","location":{"latitude":"41.67293","longitude":"-123.102185"},"name":"Unnamed Rapid","distance":"4.22","difficulty":"III+","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"","id":"TwjysEWRCljN75Qki24xZ","location":{"latitude":"41.674231","longitude":"-123.10177"},"name":"Unnamed Rapid","distance":"4.31","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"One of the larger rapids on the Scott, White House was named for the obvious white house on river left that was subsequantly painted brown. Now, the newer and much more voluminous Scott River Lodge occupies the river left, overlooking this rapid, which still goes by its original name. It's a long rapid with two boisterous breaking wave/holes (or just waves or just holes, depending on flow) at the bottom. Most boaters run down the right side but there's a left line too.","id":"HupERZbvebgaRnBtyemhh","location":{"latitude":"41.679182","longitude":"-123.101226"},"name":"White House","distance":"4.66","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"A fun rapid just downstream of Tompkins Creek, a smaller tributary that enters on river left. Nice flat rocks make a good lunch spot.","id":"TgR21tGT8MkpdlxQn01pE","location":{"latitude":"41.680596","longitude":"-123.097634"},"name":"Tompkins Creek Rapid","distance":"4.88","difficulty":"III+","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"At higher flows, this rapid has some fun big waves.","id":"jZyB1Z5F6ynG3b9W5rpO1","location":{"latitude":"41.68293","longitude":"-123.091563"},"name":"Gold Flat Rapid","distance":"5.24","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"access","description":"One of the many signed access points along the river, not many boaters use Gold Flat since the good whitewater continues downstream. The access provides easy access to a fun playspot (requires a short upstream attainment to reach) and local Scott River boaters have used this access for park &amp; play.","id":"3jaL5e4AM4Yfvw3HjxZZG","location":{"latitude":"41.684582","longitude":"-123.090269"},"name":"Gold Flat River Access","distance":"5.39","photoID":"sgeWPdfqTr9PE6yYMl00g","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"Can be used as a take-out to cut the run short before reaching Shuler Gulch Rapid. The river access is signed along Scott River Road.","id":"VZMu271VFEj7wbcoEKZga","location":{"latitude":"41.687592","longitude":"-123.084239"},"name":"Townsend Gulch River Access","distance":"5.75","photoID":"JnL9qiPYLTO7AVCkeBN0O","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"The last of the large rapids on the Scott.","id":"106859","location":{"latitude":"41.698975","longitude":"-123.039607"},"name":"Schuler Gulch Rapid","distance":"8.51","difficulty":"IV","approximate":true,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"access","description":"It's possible to take out in the little town of Scott Bar, using the county road bridge right-of-way for legal access (everything else is private property). If you're looking for a few more miles of Class II/III or a less urban take-out, continue past Scott Bar to take out at Steelhead or a couple miles further at Sarah Totten Campground on the Klamath.","id":"095ZTCt85jIeiZOsyTmL7","location":{"latitude":"41.742847","longitude":"-123.00295"},"name":"Scott Bar Bridge","distance":"12.9","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"Steelhead was once the name of the area around the confluence of the Scott and Klamath. Most of the river accessible land here is now owned by a mining club and occupied by RVs. Make nice and you may be allowed to take out here or use the right-of-way at the Highway 96 bridge or continue downriver on the Klamath to Sarah Totten Campground to get out.","id":"113084","location":{"latitude":"41.778517","longitude":"-123.03703"},"name":"Steelhead Take-Out","distance":"16.55","photoID":"m1szxeltWc4DMikWAiarb","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"sgeWPdfqTr9PE6yYMl00g","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Gold Flat River Access Sign","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"3jaL5e4AM4Yfvw3HjxZZG","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:42.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/sgeWPdfqTr9PE6yYMl00g","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/sgeWPdfqTr9PE6yYMl00g"}},{"id":"J7DabvXRJxNE85fIGj0Kd","ext":"jpg","post_id":"By0JF2U4WmXgkvvXbgOgN","description":"View of Marble Mountain, Marble Gap (Ish-ne-qua-ish), and the Kings Castle from the Scott River near Isinglass Creek in its upper reaches of the canyon.","caption":"Scot River & Marble Mountain","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Scott Harding","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2000-01-01T19:37:36.000Z","updated_at":"2025-03-04T19:37:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/J7DabvXRJxNE85fIGj0Kd","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/J7DabvXRJxNE85fIGj0Kd"}},{"id":"Ixm6RSzBRdGOEI7lRtfnq","ext":"jpg","post_id":"By0JF2U4WmXgkvvXbgOgN","description":"The Scott River between Indian Scotty and Boulder Creek Falls.","caption":"Scott River below Indian Scotty","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Scott Harding","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2000-01-01T19:37:36.000Z","updated_at":"2025-03-04T19:37:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/Ixm6RSzBRdGOEI7lRtfnq","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/Ixm6RSzBRdGOEI7lRtfnq"}},{"id":"aAZcpL24oFLxs7J4pJglp","ext":"jpg","post_id":"By0JF2U4WmXgkvvXbgOgN","description":"Scott River at Indian Scotty Campground after some rain.","caption":"Scott River at Indian Scotty","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Scott Harding","poi_id":"DWu47ZO0gWntrTWOuBAVb","photo_date":"2000-01-01T19:37:36.000Z","updated_at":"2025-03-04T19:37:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/aAZcpL24oFLxs7J4pJglp","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/aAZcpL24oFLxs7J4pJglp"}},{"id":"fG1oLP1dCHG7uFhUh6ER4","ext":"jpg","post_id":"By0JF2U4WmXgkvvXbgOgN","description":"Twilight on the Scott River in the upper reaches of its canyon.","caption":"Twilight on Scott River","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Scott Harding","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2000-01-01T19:37:36.000Z","updated_at":"2025-03-04T19:37:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/fG1oLP1dCHG7uFhUh6ER4","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/fG1oLP1dCHG7uFhUh6ER4"}},{"id":"FrDjxj17xpYwASqo48Uh2","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"4fvmJquVCKFYtCyNuyg2w","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:43.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/FrDjxj17xpYwASqo48Uh2","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/FrDjxj17xpYwASqo48Uh2"}},{"id":"m1szxeltWc4DMikWAiarb","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Confluence Where Scott Joins the Klamath","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:42.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/m1szxeltWc4DMikWAiarb","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/m1szxeltWc4DMikWAiarb"}},{"id":"1eGD0HSi725yojlPHOVqZ","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Lower Part of Boulder Creek Falls","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"94weRpF2IxgnsQyNyaQ5A","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:42.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1eGD0HSi725yojlPHOVqZ","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1eGD0HSi725yojlPHOVqZ"}},{"id":"OKGFgts7VHHlzIZPzDCrP","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Lower Part of Boulder Creek Falls","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"94weRpF2IxgnsQyNyaQ5A","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:43.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/OKGFgts7VHHlzIZPzDCrP","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/OKGFgts7VHHlzIZPzDCrP"}},{"id":"Ewqo6QBTPCvkdHpXeao5B","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Scott River Canyon from the Shuttle","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:43.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/Ewqo6QBTPCvkdHpXeao5B","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/Ewqo6QBTPCvkdHpXeao5B"}},{"id":"cmuy0FdE5GigKx3yQNJgZ","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"aPg3yYBoF8H4Rd4ipc1By","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:43.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/cmuy0FdE5GigKx3yQNJgZ","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/cmuy0FdE5GigKx3yQNJgZ"}},{"id":"FzIP5mRfEeMniI3V4Fbme","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Scott River Canyon from the Shuttle","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:42.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/FzIP5mRfEeMniI3V4Fbme","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/FzIP5mRfEeMniI3V4Fbme"}},{"id":"krBJrSF4gihaohgcbdp3u","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Boulder Creek Access","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"25KP9uYdE6pvHUT0uQOhp","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:42.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/krBJrSF4gihaohgcbdp3u","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/krBJrSF4gihaohgcbdp3u"}},{"id":"tkSPWUP1Gl6brWc8VTfmh","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Boulder Creek Falls","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"94weRpF2IxgnsQyNyaQ5A","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:43.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/tkSPWUP1Gl6brWc8VTfmh","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/tkSPWUP1Gl6brWc8VTfmh"}},{"id":"JnL9qiPYLTO7AVCkeBN0O","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Townsend Gulch River Access Sign","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"VZMu271VFEj7wbcoEKZga","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:42.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/JnL9qiPYLTO7AVCkeBN0O","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/JnL9qiPYLTO7AVCkeBN0O"}},{"id":"I9WVb5Oj3mGbaxmrdw46M","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Lower Part of Boulder Creek Falls","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"94weRpF2IxgnsQyNyaQ5A","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:43.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/I9WVb5Oj3mGbaxmrdw46M","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/I9WVb5Oj3mGbaxmrdw46M"}},{"id":"UGUZYX3Bs6CUaXPchq97r","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Sugar Pine River Access Sign","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:42.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/UGUZYX3Bs6CUaXPchq97r","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/UGUZYX3Bs6CUaXPchq97r"}},{"id":"M5QqYeupCYy0gO2ovdAnh","ext":"jpg","post_id":"fpftbRbjql5txGNunCwHD","description":"","caption":"Confluence Where Scott Joins the Klamath","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe and Rie Yamazaki","poi_id":"113084","photo_date":"2025-04-06T04:58:42.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-15T04:58:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/M5QqYeupCYy0gO2ovdAnh","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/M5QqYeupCYy0gO2ovdAnh"}},{"id":"18837","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9738","description":"","caption":"18837.jpeg","poi_name":"Schulers Gulch","subject":"Devin Knight","author":"Darin McQuoid","poi_id":"106859","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18837","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18837"}}],"detail":{"id":"295","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"The Scott River is a whitewater gem in far Northern California that was first boated in 1969, making it one of the earlier boating runs in this area. Originating in the Scott Mountains of the Trinity Alps Wilderness and the Salmon Mountains of the Russian and Marble Mountain wildernesses, it flows through the pastoral Scott Valley before plunging into a rugged horseshoe-shaped canyon as it wraps around the Scott Bar Mountains. For more than 20 miles, the Scott delivers varied and exciting whitewater before joining the Klamath River at Steelhead, California.\n\nThe Scott River Canyon is very scenic too. From a point on the river just upstream of Deep Creek, the upriver view to Boulder Peak rises more than 6,000 vertical feet above the river. There are some gorgeous rocks in the river, particularly near Kelsey Creek, where swirled marble boulders have washed down from Marble Mountain and the Kings Castle. The downriver end of the canyon shows signs of wildfires on both sides of the river, most recently from the 2022 [McKinney Fire](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McKinney_Fire).\n\nDespite its spectacular setting and excellent whitewater, the Scott is often overlooked, partly due to its brown, sediment-laden water from valley runoff—unlike the typically clear, emerald-green rivers and streams nearby. Catching it at good flows is also challenging, as a drying climate and agricultural diversions in Scott Valley have shortened its season and volume considerably, sometimes even eliminating a boating season altogether. The valley’s alluvial aquifer, heavily drawn for irrigation, must recharge through rains and snowmelt before the river downstream will reach boatable flows.\n\nThe Scott offers a number of diverse runs downstream of Scott Valley: 1) a Class II/III section in the upper canyon that is rarely boated, 2) a Class IV/V reach below Jones Beach featuring the highly technical Boulder Creek Falls, 3) the more commonly run Class IV stretch from Bridge Flat to Scott Bar, and 4) a few miles of Class II from there to the Klamath River. Multiple access points, both signed and unsigned, provide options for boaters. While much of the canyon is public land managed by Klamath National Forest, numerous private properties exist along the river, especially near Scott Bar.\n\n**Flows &amp; Season**\n\nThe Scott River’s hydrology is heavily impacted by its history of post-settlement development and, in particular, by ongoing agricultural diversions and groundwater pumping in Scott Valley, compounded by a drying climate and declining snowpack. It once had reliable winter and spring flows, predictable and regualr enough to support several whitewater rafting outfitters. However, since the early 2000s, boatable flows have become increasingly rare and unpredictable. While nearby streams a fraction of the Scott’s size may run at 1,500 cfs, the Scott itself often trickles by at a mere 100 cfs. It doesn’t reach boatable flows every year, but in wet years, it can run fairly regualrly—feast or famine.\n\nIn good years, the Scott may start running in late December or January, usually after warm rain with a snowmelt component. If the valley’s depleted aquifer recharges during winter then late winter and spring rains and snowmelt are more likely to produce sustained boatable flows. As spring arrives with warmer temperatures and less rain, the river transitions from rainfall-driven flows to a greater reliance on snowmelt. If winter has been wet and snowpack is solid, a spring runoff season in April and May can occur—though agricultural diversions and groundwater pumping resume on April 15. In exceptional years, the Scott may even run into late May or early June.\n\nMore often, however, the Scott fails to reach boatable flows until late winter or spring, if at all. When it does, the window is short, with flows spiking and dropping rapidly, usually related to a storm. Watch the gage and be ready to seize the opportunity. Water quality can also be poor in these years due to a lack of flushing flows. If flows are too low, nearby alternatives include the Salmon River, the creeks near Happy Camp, or the Klamath.\n\nOnce in the cell-free Scott River canyon, you can check the latest flow reading using the public phones at the Bridge Flat River Access or in Scott Bar, which also has free WiFi at the post office. Call [Waterline](http://www.h2oline.com/default.aspx?pg=st&op=CA) at 1-800-452-1737 and enter 063492 for the Scott River near Fort Jones. The USGS gauge is located at the upstream end of the canyon.\n\nThe USGS gage sits at the top of the canyon, upstream of the primary boating runs. Tributaries like Canyon, Boulder, Kelsey, and Middle creeks—flowing in from river left—add significant water below the gage, meaning boaters downstream of Jones Beach should expect higher flows than the reading provides.\n\n**Logistics**\n\n*Put-ins*\n\n[Jones Beach](https://www.fs.usda.gov/recarea/klamath/recarea/?recid=13142) is the furthest upriver national forest river access site and is easy to find along Scott River Road just inside the Klamath National Forest. A paved parking area leads to a trail down to a sandy beach on river right. Launching here provides a warm-up before the action-packed section downstream, which includes Boulder Creek Falls (a solid V) and Canyon Creek Rapid (IV).\n\nHowever, most boaters choose to avoid Boulder Creek Falls and put in below the falls either at Buker Bridge or, more commonly, at [Bridge Flat River Access](https://www.fs.usda.gov/recarea/klamath/recarea/?recid=13140). This eliminates some excellent whitewater but avoids the challenge and drama of Boulder Creek Falls, which is suited only for expert kayakers who like tight, congested drops.\n\n*Take-outs*\n\nIt's possible to take out in the little town of Scott Bar, using the county road bridge right-of-way for legal access (everything else is private property). If you're looking for a few more miles of Class II/III or a less urban take-out, continue past Scott Bar to take out at Steelhead, just upstream of Highway 96 and the Klamath River confluence.\n\nRegardless of what put-in and take-out you choose, Scott River Road follows the river’s length, though at times it runs high above the canyon, with no direct river access. The Scott is an easy river to bike shuttle and the one-lane paved road is pretty quiet and very scenic.\n\n**Additional Info**\n\nThe best guidebook for this run is Dan Menten's 2016 classic, *The New School Guide to Northern California Whitewater,* p. 55.\n\nOther books: Cassady &amp; Calhoun, Holbek &amp; Stanley (p. 68), Schwind","ave